infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title JSON-array column vs relational join table
  desc Same "user has many tags" requirement — the shape you pick decides which queries are point lookups and which scan every row
  compares
    - label JSON-array / CSV column
      children
        - label "One user's tags" is fast
          desc The array lives on the row, so a single point read returns it
        - label "Who has tag X" scans every row
          desc Every array must be parsed; "how many users have tag X" hits the same wall
        - label "Rename tag X to Y" rewrites every row
          desc The value is duplicated inline, so the engine cannot index it from either direction
        - label No constraints
          desc Application bugs commit orphan or duplicate tags — a key-value store with SQL syntax
    - label Relational join table
      children
        - label All three queries become point lookups
          desc A user_tags table is indexable from either direction, ~1000x cheaper to query
        - label Composite PK blocks duplicates
          desc (user_id, item_id) PRIMARY KEY refuses the same pair twice
        - label Two foreign keys block orphans
          desc The engine refuses any row pointing at a non-existent user or tag
        - label Cost is two extra schema lines
          desc Schema discipline up front; correctness every caller gets for free
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
